to see you this morning these numbers  continuing to go  in the wrong direction minister despite  that the tears have been in for  for quite some time now what's going  wrong  well as the health secretary said this  week the rate of growth  has slowed but still we're seeing the  virus  uptick the most important thing is to  keep targeting the virus  where it is picking up where it is  gathering momentum  which is why the targeted localized  focused approach  not just with restrictions but with the  financial support is the right approach  and that's the way we'll avoid  the rather blanket blunt uh tool of a  national lockdown which  i think is right to avoid the who has  said uh  should only be used in a reserve measure  but also from the  economic point of view it will be very  damaging so we're  but what's what's most important at this  point in time is everyone leans into  this  so where targeted measures are in place  we've got them in my own constituency we  follow the guidance meticulously  we've got local leaders uh cooperating  very closely  with the government which we've  overwhelmingly seen  mostly across the country there have  been some exceptions but mostly that  collaboration has been strong  that's the way we avoid the the rather  blunt tool of a national lockdown  there is an argument to say though that  obviously these these tier restrictions  are going to be  in place for a lot longer than the short  sharp shock of a national lockdown would  need to be to  to have the same effect so even in  economic terms  a national lockdown may make more sense  well if you look at those who've  advocated a national lockdown including  kyostama  for example but others they've never  been able to say how you get out of it  so  the idea of a short sharp circuit  breaker is frankly  uh something of an enigma no one can say  if you go into a national lockdown how  you at what point you get out of it so i  i think that would not only be damaging  it wouldn't be in economic terms it  wouldn't be the targeted approach  in epidemiological terms and it would  create great uncertainty  so we appreciate this is difficult i  know from my own constituency  the challenges the disruption the  inconvenience of being in the height  here  um but it's better to take a targeted  approach than have the blunt tool  of nationwide restrictions at the very  highest  possible level which i think a lot of  people in those areas where the virus  hasn't taken hold  would think was unfair as well as  ineffective yeah but it's it's  interesting though  you you quote sakia stamina as being one  of those in favor of a national lockdown  well sage your scientific advisory body  was in favor of a national lockdown  it can't be dismissed  no it can't be dismissed it can be  argued and tested and of course  we had the full range of options  presented by the scientists to the  government  uh with chris whittie with patrick  valance the chief scientific advisor the  chief  medical officer and ultimately of course  politicians need to take responsibility  but overwhelmingly uh our senses and i  and i think this is backed up for  example with what the world health  organization  has said is that you're far better  leaning in being absolutely committed to  targeted localized uh measures with the  financial support  than taking the blanket blunt approach  of a nationwide lockdown  if you can possibly help it so it's  absolutely right  that we strain every sinew work with  local leaders provide financial support  but target the virus where the uptick is  the most threatening and that's the  approach we're taking  we've just been talking to sir mark  walport who's as you will know is a  member of  of the sage committee and he he has more  sense than to  to tread on the toes of policy makers he  wouldn't dare do that but he did point  out  that you know throughout all of this  we've we've tend to have been about a  month behind  at mainland europe and he does point out  that france  and germany are entering national  lockdowns we see the situation in  ireland in wales of course  his his the indication is that that it  just may become necessary at some point  well the advantage in the situation you  described and us  being able to follow uh the the  situation in other countries if you like  is that we can learn the lessons from it  and the fundamental  key lesson is to lean into the localized  targeted focused measures and to make  them as effective as as possible  precisely because if we don't there is  that risk  of a nationwide lockdown which will be  damaging and is not the most targeted  way of tackling the virus so i think  you're right to point to that  i talk to my french german and  opposite numbers and around the world uh  we look to learn from the lessons and  the experience  but i think the targeted approach the  localized approach is the right way to  go but you've really got to lean in and  be committed to it  and that of course is the advice of  people like patrick valance and chris  whitty and the scientist  as well as i think making economic sense  as well  i'm interested that you said in terms of  a national lockdown no one can say how  you would come out of it  well how do you come out of tier 3  well with all of the targeted approaches  the advantage we've got is we would  examine the data  locally so there is a review process in  place  for all the tiered localized  restrictions that we've got  it allows us to look not just at  abstract national level  data but at the specific data in the  communities  and the regions affected and of course  both based on the prevalence but also  the growth rate we are able to track in  a much more  granular way what the virus is doing and  so that allows us  to ease off uh when we've made the  progress that we need to see  when the threat is abated so we we've  got we're in a much better position  to to address the point you raised to be  able to exit in a targeted responsible  way  when you take localized restrictions  precisely because we now measure the  virus on a localized basis  the government's in an unenviable an  enviable position  no one with any sense is is going to  disagree  with that you're trying to balance the  health of the nation as well as the  economic health of the nation  um is there an acknowledgement though  that things have not always  worked to plan i mean you eat out to  help out was was so  widely applauded businesses liked it  though the  concerns the economic benefit was pretty  short-lived the university of warwick's  study out today  says it drove up spikes of covert 19  infections by between 8 and 17  i mean do we have to acknowledge that as  a scheme it actually caused some  problems  i'm not sure that i would accept that  analysis but of course i'd look at  uh any evidence that comes but you're  right to say that there's a global  pandemic  we learn the lessons as we go with any  virus which is new  and of course one of the big things  compared to where we were in february  march is that we've got a huge amount  more learning  the testing and tracing capacity that  we've got is now  close to five hundred thousand per day  so uh  we in a stronger position in that  respect we we know more about the virus  we've made the preparations for the nhs  we saw in the first lockdown we  preserved  uh nhs capacity we continue to work very  hard at that  we we can also take the economic  measures to provide people businesses  and employees uh  self-employed with the support that they  need so we you're absolutely right to  say that we will learn  all the time we learn in this country we  learn the lessons from further afield  across  uh across the world and that's the right  approach i think that's what  people would expect i just wonder if i'm  wondering quite how to phrase this  minister but it about it's about  carrying the people with you  isn't it in all of this you did that  very well  during the national lockdown people you  know really sort of obeyed the rules  in the main and we saw you know a very  big improvement in the figures  and i know ministers like yourself have  talked to um don't know how many of you  over the last few months and you all say  well most people are  following the rules you only have to  walk out to  any high street any shopping center and  you see  that is not the case people aren't  behind this anymore  how do you get people back in step with  what needs to be done  well i do accept that as things uh  continue uh people's become people  become weary  with the state of play but i also think  there is a very strong latent sense of  understanding that we're in  an unprecedented global pandemic which  the likes of which we've never seen  before and  and of course it's the job of the media  to pick up on one or other area where  things aren't going well and  and it's right that our feet are held to  the fire but i can tell you i'll be out  in my constituency  uh uh down in uh stoked avenue on the  high street i  do this every week talking to retailers  talking to businesses  and actually i the thing that we hear is  that we understand  this is an incredibly difficult  situation but the gov and the  government's gonna get criticized  whatever it does  but there's a lot of understanding that  uh the support is being put in place and  the approach that we're taking  is the right one and overwhelmingly  people  are following the rules of course  there's some weariness with it of course  there's some testing at the edges and as  rules change  the the challenge is to keep the clarity  of message but i think the country does  understand the predicament we're in  and people do not want to end up in a  national lockdown so the number one  thing to do right now  is in whatever local area you're in  follow the guidance follow the rules the  government's providing the support  that's the way we get through this and  we will get through this there will be  light at the end of the tunnel but but  it's it's about  guidance being clear yeah i have to say  from from a personal point of view i got  annoyed  during lockdown when people said the  guidance wasn't clear because it seemed  pretty clear to me  when we're in national lockdown it's not  clear to me now  i mean it's very confusing i mean every  tier three area has got different sets  of  rules there needs to be some proper  proper  clarity here which doesn't seem to just  doesn't seem to be there at the moment  well i don't agree with that or although  i understand the the challenge  that people have but the reality is you  can go online and check  absolutely precisely and definitively in  your area  what the restrictions are but if we  followed your advice stephen and i take  it very seriously  um we'd end up with uh for the sake of  clarity with a one-size-fits-all  approach  that wouldn't be the most effective way  of tackling the virus and what we've got  to do with  laser-like focus is is concentrate on  how we get the virus down and that is a  targeted approach a focused approach  yes i understand we need to have a clear  message but i think people do understand  that  the the that we've got to be take a more  nuanced approach  uh where the virus is the strongest and  that's the thing that will pull us  through  and we will get through this uh we will  get through this with the testing and  tracing that we're ramping up  with the scope for vaccine and  therapeutics the drugs that  take the reduce the fatality rate of  this disease  and we just got to stick with it and  it's tough and it's wearing and i  appreciate the impact on people's lives  and their livelihoods but we will get  through this and  i'm confident the approach the  government has taken the targeted  approach  the focused approach is the right one

